**MultiTracking With Logic Pro**
As an audio engineer I use a plethora of Digital Audio Workstations, better known as DAW’s. For school work, Pro Tools tends to be the main choice. Primarily due to its standardization in the industry. But for personal use I like to use Logic Pro. Being that they all are built off of a base conceptual model, they are easily interchangeable. 

The DAW was developed as a way to improve the work flow of studio recording. Before the adaptation of the DAW, audio was recorded onto analog tape. Which meant there was a limit to the manipulation of audio and how many tracks and takes could be recorded, on one project. As a modern musician DAW literacy and being able to record oneself is a must.

When recording into a DAW, for this example I am using Logic Pro It's important that you set the amount of channels you require, being that I am recording drums for a demo, I chose a total of 7 mic inputs, by default it sets the inputs to cascade starting at my input of choice. Being that this is a required step in getting a project started it is prompted upon opening the software. Shown below is an example of the starting prompt page.

My goal was to input a track that was sent to me by my guitarist. Based on standard file management Conventions I opened up my files and dragged the file into my Logic session. Based on the consistency and Standards of most DAWs it worked with no issue, and now I can get started recording. 

When looking over the softwares main window page, I can see the audio file for my backing tracks, placed below the 7 tracks I have laid out for my drums. To arm tracks that I want to record I know I need to manually input it. The Affordance of a big red R button on every track made me feel like that was the reasonable solution. Now that my selected tracks are flashing red, I need to start my recordings. Pressing the space bar just plays the tracks and doesn’t record enable. Towards the top of the menu where the tool bar resides, there's a red circle next to the play button which implies its use for recording. When I finished recording I hit the space bar and it turned off my record enable and paused my session. 
  	
When it comes to mixing the final results, the natural mapping of the mixing board in Logic Pro is a Match Between the System and the Real World, because it follows the similar formatting of an analog console, replicating a lot of the same work flow. The mapping of the faders indicate that they control the corresponding channel strip's volume, and the bouncing green light helps indicate the volume of the sound. Typically at the top of an analog channel strip you will have an EQ, dynamic processor, and aux send. These characteristics are mirrored on the DAW and have very few limitations to how they can be used compared to their analog counterparts, furthermore showing the versatility of DAWs. 

